  "prompt_text": "The figure contrasts two optical environments for emitters and links their spectral density to how multiple emitters interact. On the left, the density of states \\(D(\\omega)\\) is sketched for a cavity versus a waveguide. The cavity shows a narrow, sharp resonance centered at one frequency, indicating that emission is strongly enhanced only near that resonant mode. The waveguide instead shows a broad allowed band with strong features near its edges, suggesting a much wider frequency range of available modes.\n\nOn the right, these spectra are translated into collective behavior of several emitters placed above a structured surface. In the “coherent” case, the emitters seem to couple through overlapping, extended optical fields spread across the surface, implying long-range, phase-preserving interactions. In the “dissipative” case, each emitter appears to radiate more locally into the structure, with energy transferred through lossy guided excitations along the surface, emphasizing decay and damping rather than coherent exchange.\n\nOverall, the figure’s main point is that the photonic environment—narrow resonant cavity mode versus broad waveguide continuum—determines whether emitter interactions are predominantly resonant and coherent or broadband and dissipative.",
